now I  don't know and I so I thought about it I  guess for a second although I was  completely in shock and  um and I said well  if it's not going to work and  why put him through all of that  let's just stop  um and by the way  um the reason why they were asking me  what I wanted to do was because I had a  marriage certificate which is why it's  so important I had a legal document  saying that I was his partner that I was  his husband  um  otherwise they would not have let me  into the ICU  just by the way  uh so I said okay I guess we should stop  so as soon as I said that literally they  were like okay let's go and so a room  full of like doctors and nurses  ran out of the room and they took all of  the um  took everything with them they took the  monitors they they unhooked things they  gone the only thing that was left was a  ventilator because he had been on a  ventilator  um because his uh his organs had started  shutting down his his liver  shut down and his kidneys were about to  shut down they were talking about at one  point during that day putting him on  dialysis  and all this stuff  but at any rate as soon as I said okay  let's stop they were gone  the only thing left was the ventilator  so he was breathing  and  I didn't know what to do and there was  this very kind nurse  who saw that I was just like  just like stunned and didn't know what  to do and she said talk to him  she said because somewhere somewhere in  there he can hear you okay  so I got into bed I crawled into bed  with him and I held him  and I started talking to him and I told  him how much I loved him  and I told him that I said don't worry  about me  because I'll be okay  and I said and I said don't be afraid  because whatever is going to happen to  you now is going to be  great and fascinating and  probably wonderful and  and I held I just held him  um  and he was on the ventilator like I said  so he kept breathing  but even though he was breathing  there was a very clear moment  where I knew that he was gone  that he had died  I could feel it  it became very clear to me in an instant  that he was gone  that the  person I knew that Antonio was gone and  that what was here with me at that  moment  was just his body  it was just very clear to me  even though he was still breathing  and  so I stayed there for a long time  nobody came nobody bothered me they just  let me stay there as long as I wanted  until I finally  I don't know how long I was there uh but  then I finally thought okay  it's time for me to go  so I left  um  and then I went home  and  um this might possibly have been the  worst part of it in fact I went home and  of course there was a Christmas tree  with all of our Christmas gifts  so it took me many years in fact after  that for me to be able to enjoy  Christmas again because that you know  um  and I remember I sat in my apartment for  probably  two weeks  and I don't remember eating  I know I lost a lot of weight  um I I think I was  I must have been in shock I was just I  was just completely stunned  um  I remember it must maybe two or three  weeks later  I I remember this very distinctly  sort of getting up  it was very hard to sleep I maybe slept  two maybe three hours a day  mostly I just sat and just kind of like  it was in my head  I remember there was there was this one  day where I I stood up and I looked out  the window of our apartment and I saw  there were people outside they were they  were human beings like walking around  and and there were cars driving by  and  I remember very distinctly thinking  like something something is wrong  something is really wrong people don't  you understand like what are you doing  the world has ended  how are you outside like how are you  walking how like how are you having  lives how are you talking to each other  I didn't understand because  in my mind the world had ended  um  so I did that for a long time  um  and then finally of course  um I realized at one point maybe a  couple of months later I realized that I  really needed help  um so somehow I found a a a grief  Therapy Group I found a group of people  there were eight of us in this group  um who all had lost spouses around the  same time so they put this this place  they put this uh group together  the place is called our house here in  Los Angeles and they put this group  together of eight people and we  committed to a year and a half of grief  therapy so we went through this whole  grief  process for a year and a half together  which was tremendous  tremendously helpful  um  and  I told them the story of course  and  of course they all wanted to hear the  poem  which I still have  in my wallet  a little piece of paper  the poem is called wait for him  wait for the guy who calls you beautiful  instead of hot  who calls you back when you hang up on  him who stays awake just to watch you  sleep  who worries and takes care of you when  you are ill  wait for the guy who kisses your  forehead  who wants to show you off to the world  when you're wearing sweats  who holds your hand in front of his  friends  wait for the guy who is constantly  reminding you of how much he cares about  you and how lucky he is to have you  wait for the guy who turns to his  friends and says  that's him  wait for the guy who only wants you  and his name was uh Antonio Mojica and  when we got married he took my name so  his name was Antonio Mojica Vaccaro  and  it took me a long time  I think to be able to  live in the world again like a normal  human being  um  I remember this very distinctly it was  about two years later  two years two full years  when I laughed for the very first time  that I could remember since his death  I laughed I laughed out loud at  something I don't remember what I  laughed at but I laughed out loud and it  was this laugh that  you know that had been waiting two years  to come out of me  and I was so filled with joy at the idea  that I laughed but that only lasted a  second  um  because then I felt horrible for  laughing I thought  I can't possibly laugh I can't possibly  enjoy life ever again because then that  would be a betrayal if I wasn't  miserable for the rest of my life that  would be a betrayal to him  um but it took me a while to realize  that that of course was  insane  um  and that I had to figure out a way to  be a happy person to be a happy normal  person  and so that it's been quite a few years  now  um of course I still think about him  every single day  I still  um miss him  I went through a very long period of  time when I was where I would be home  for instance and I would I lived in an  apartment building I would be home and I  would hear somebody some keys jangling  out the hallway  and I would immediately I would think  oh  Antonio's home  or there would be times where something  something really wonderful would happen  in my life I'd get some great job or  something or something really fun  happened and my first thought was to  pick up my phone and call Antonio was  like  oh I can't right still happens to me all  these years later  uh but I have figured out how to  um get maneuver through life and be a  happy person and I realized that this  grief  will always be a part of me we'll always  be a part of who I am always and and  that's really fine  I'm really okay with that  so I met Antonio on  April 28th  2009 and he died exactly we got married  exactly four months later  August 28th and he died on December 28th  we had exactly eight months together  uh but it was it was eight months of  living with a rock star  and it will always be a part of me  that's my story  beautiful
 

